Here is an iterative version using a stack, so it does not use recursive function calls. Each popped node is checked locally, and its non-null children are pushed for later.

Dry run for a small tree with root `10`, left child `4`, and right child `6`: the root passes because `4 + 6` is `10`. Both children are leaves, so they also pass. The final result is `true`.

```cpp
/* class TreeNode {
       int val;
       TreeNode *left, *right;
       TreeNode(int x) : val(x), left(NULL), right(NULL) {}
   };
*/

class Solution {
public:
    bool checkChildrenSum(TreeNode* root) {
        if (root == nullptr) {
            return true;
        }

        TreeNode* stack[100000];
        int top = 0;
        stack[top++] = root;

        while (top > 0) {
            TreeNode* current = stack[--top];

            if (current->left == nullptr && current->right == nullptr) {
                continue;
            }

            int total = 0;
            if (current->left != nullptr) {
                total += current->left->val;
            }
            if (current->right != nullptr) {
                total += current->right->val;
            }

            if (current->val != total) {
                return false;
            }

            if (current->right != nullptr) {
                stack[top++] = current->right;
            }
            if (current->left != nullptr) {
                stack[top++] = current->left;
            }
        }

        return true;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.

Space complexity: `O(n)` for the explicit stack array. The input tree is read only and remains unchanged.

The empty tree and a tree containing only one leaf both return `true`.

The condition only applies to nodes that have at least one child. The method below keeps the leaf check separate, which makes the one-child case easy to read: the absent child has value zero.

No input tree is changed by this code.

```java
