class Solution {
    boolean checkChildrenSum(TreeNode root) {
        if (root == null || (root.left == null && root.right == null)) {
            return true;
        }

        int childrenTotal = 0;
        if (root.left != null) {
            childrenTotal += root.left.val;
        }
        if (root.right != null) {
            childrenTotal += root.right.val;
        }

        if (root.val != childrenTotal) {
            return false;
        }

        boolean leftValid = checkChildrenSum(root.left);
        boolean rightValid = checkChildrenSum(root.right);
        return leftValid && rightValid;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.

Space complexity: `O(h)` due to DFS recursion. For a skewed tree, the stack can grow to `O(n)`.

The helper returns whether the subtree rooted at a node follows the rule. It first checks both children, then checks the current node. This makes an invalid child fail the parent immediately.

For example, with a node `10` and children `4` and `6`, the local check is `10 == 4 + 6`, so that node passes. A leaf returns `True` without trying to access child values.

```python
class Solution:
    def checkChildrenSum(self, root: TreeNode) -> bool:
        def valid(node):
            if node is None:
                return True

            if node.left is None and node.right is None:
                return True

            left_value = node.left.val if node.left is not None else 0
            right_value = node.right.val if node.right is not None else 0

            current_ok = node.val == left_value + right_value
            if not current_ok:
                return False

            return valid(node.left) and valid(node.right)

        return valid(root)
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` because each node is visited at most once.

Space complexity: `O(h)` for recursion, with `h` equal to the height of the tree.

Edge cases covered here include an empty tree, a single-node tree, and nodes with only one child.

This version visits the tree level by level. The queue stores nodes that still need to be checked. For every internal node, its value must equal the sum of its left and right child values. The null child case is handled as zero.

A null root is also valid, and a leaf is simply added to the queue without any comparison.

```java
// class TreeNode {
//     int val;
//     TreeNode left, right;
//     TreeNode(int x) { val = x; }
// }

class Solution {
    boolean checkChildrenSum(TreeNode root) {
        if (root == null) {
            return true;
        }

        java.util.ArrayDeque<TreeNode> queue = new java.util.ArrayDeque<>();
        queue.offer(root);

        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
            TreeNode current = queue.poll();

            if (current.left == null && current.right == null) {
                continue;
            }

            int leftValue = current.left == null ? 0 : current.left.val;
            int rightValue = current.right == null ? 0 : current.right.val;

            if (current.val != leftValue + rightValue) {
                return false;
            }

            if (current.left != null) {
                queue.offer(current.left);
            }
            if (current.right != null) {
                queue.offer(current.right);
            }
        }

        return true;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.

Space complexity: `O(w)`, where `w` is the largest level in the tree. This can be `O(n)` for a wide tree.

A direct recursive check is enough here. For each non-leaf node, add the values of its existing children and compare that total with the node value. A missing child contributes `0`. Leaf nodes are already valid because they have no children to check.

