class Solution {
public:
    bool checkChildrenSum(TreeNode* root) {
        if (root == NULL || 
            (root->left == NULL && root->right == NULL))
            return true;

        int total = 0;

        if (root->left)
            total += root->left->val;

        if (root->right)
            total += root->right->val;

        return root->val == total &&
               checkChildrenSum(root->left) &&
               checkChildrenSum(root->right);
    }
};
```

## Complexity

**Time Complexity:** `O(N)`

Every node is visited exactly once.

**Space Complexity:** `O(H)`

The recursion stack takes `O(H)` space, where `H` is the height of the tree.

* Balanced tree: `O(log N)`
* Skewed tree: `O(N)`

## Intuition
The Children Sum Property requires that every non-leaf node has a value equal to the sum of its children's values. For a tree to be valid, this condition must hold at the current node and recursively across both left and right subtrees. Leaf nodes and empty nodes trivially satisfy the condition

## Approach
Handle base cases: return true if root is nullptr or if it is a leaf node (both children are nullptr).

Calculate the expected sum by checking if the left child exists, if the right child exists, or if both exist.

Check if root->val equals the sum of its children.

Recursively pass the check to both root->left and root->right using logical && so that if any subtree fails, the whole tree returns false.

## Solution
```cpp[]
class Solution {
public:
    bool fun(TreeNode* root) {
        if(root == nullptr)
            return true;

        if(root->left == nullptr && root->right == nullptr)
            return true;

        if(root->left == nullptr)
            return root->val == root->right->val && fun(root->right);

        if(root->right == nullptr)
            return root->val == root->left->val && fun(root->left);

        return root->val == root->left->val + root->right->val
            && fun(root->left)
            && fun(root->right);
    }

    bool checkChildrenSum(TreeNode* root) {
        return fun(root);
    }
};
```

Intuition
The key insight is that to validate a node, you need the values of its children first — so you process children before the parent. This is a classic post-order traversal pattern.
Algorithm

Recursively check the left subtree. If it fails, short-circuit and return false.
Recursively check the right subtree. If it fails, short-circuit and return false.
Handle the base cases:

null node → return true (vacuously valid)
Leaf node (no children) → return true (no constraint to satisfy)


For any internal node, sum up the values of whichever children exist (left, right, or both) and verify it equals root.val.

Why post-order?
If you tried top-down, you'd be checking a node before knowing whether its subtrees are valid — you'd need extra passes. Post-order gives you validity of both subtrees before you check the current node, making the logic clean and one-pass.
Complexity

Time: O(N) — every node is visited exactly once.
Space: O(H) — recursion stack, where H is the height of the tree. O(log N) for balanced, O(N) worst case (skewed tree).

